{"name":"Sugar Pie","hex":"#c7a77b","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sugar pie","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A pie, typically consisting of a crust with a filling made with cream and brown sugar (or, especially in Quebec, maple syrup), popular in northern France, Belgium, and Quebec.","Synonym of sugar cream pie."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sugar_pie"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tarte au sucre","blocks":[{"text":"Tarte au sucre is a dessert from northern French and Belgian cuisine, which is also popular in Canada."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Tarte_au_sucre.jpeg/330px-Tarte_au_sucre.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tarte_au_sucre","wikidata":"Q3515853"},{"title":"Sugar","blocks":[{"text":"Sugar is a class of sweet-tasting, soluble carbohydrates, many of which are used in food. Simple sugars, also called monosaccharides, include glucose, fructose and galactose. Compound sugars, also called disaccharides or double sugars, are molecules made of two bonded monosaccharides; common examples are sucrose, lactose and maltose. White sugar is almost pure sucrose. During digestion, compound sugars are hydrolysed into simple sugars."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Sucre_blanc_cassonade_complet_rapadura.jpg/330px-Sucre_blanc_cassonade_complet_rapadura.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sugar","wikidata":"Q11002"},{"title":"Pie","blocks":[{"text":"A pie is a baked dish which is usually made of a pastry dough casing that contains a filling of various sweet or savoury ingredients. Sweet pies may be filled with fruit, nuts, fruit preserves, brown sugar, sweetened vegetables, or with thicker fillings based on eggs and dairy. Savoury pies may be filled with meat, eggs and cheese, or a mixture of meat and vegetables."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Top_down_view_of_peach_pie_%28722379748%29.jpg/330px-Top_down_view_of_peach_pie_%28722379748%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pie","wikidata":"Q13360264"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Canaviais_Sao_Paulo_01_2008_06.jpg/500px-Canaviais_Sao_Paulo_01_2008_06.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Canaviais_Sao_Paulo_01_2008_06.jpg/1280px-Canaviais_Sao_Paulo_01_2008_06.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Canaviais_Sao_Paulo_01_2008_06.jpg/500px-Canaviais_Sao_Paulo_01_2008_06.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sugar cane plantation","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACanaviais_Sao_Paulo_01_2008_06.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Carbohydrate.png/500px-XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Carbohydrate.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Carbohydrate.png/1280px-XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Carbohydrate.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Carbohydrate.png/500px-XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Carbohydrate.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sugars (e.g. glucose) and their polymers (such as starch, cellulose).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AXBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Carbohydrate.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commo